/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain DIRT

  /* The snow half. Made here so that it exists before elevation runs. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    land_percent 45
    base_size 10
    left_border 55
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  /* The dirt half. ONE command. */
  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain DIRT
    number_of_clumps 40
    number_of_tiles 4000
  }

  /* The snow half. The SAME command, five times. */
  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain SNOW
    number_of_clumps 40
    number_of_tiles 4000
  }

  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain SNOW
    number_of_clumps 40
    number_of_tiles 4000
  }

  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain SNOW
    number_of_clumps 40
    number_of_tiles 4000
  }

  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain SNOW
    number_of_clumps 40
    number_of_tiles 4000
  }

  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain SNOW
    number_of_clumps 40
    number_of_tiles 4000
  }

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_50 - do repeated elevation commands STACK, or does each one raise to its
own height and stop?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.2, elevation.ts, Sec.15 item 25

QUESTION
Sec.6.2 said elevation heights "add onto whatever elevation is already there",
and the implementation read that as summing across commands. A corpus map then
writes the same flood-the-map elevation command five times over one region, plus
a smaller one - summed, that is a height of 37 on a scale that ceilings at 16 -
and the preview came out at the ceiling on 36,877 of 40,000 tiles for a map
whose author asked for 7.

It is now modelled as: each command raises a tile to its own target and takes
the higher of that and what is already there, so writing a command five times is
a COVERAGE idiom - exactly what repetition means for terrain - rather than a
height multiplier. The maximum reading was reached by asking what would stop the
bad output, which is the weaker way to reach a rule, so it is worth one run.

The related pin is NOT in question here and should not be disturbed: a land's
own base elevation still adds, so a land at base 3 under a command asking for 7
reaches 10. That half was measured. This test is only about repetition.

METHOD
One map, two halves of different terrain, and elevation commands restricted to a
terrain by the elevation command's own base-terrain attribute. The two halves
differ ONLY in how many times the same command is written.

THE TWO HALVES ARE MADE AT LAND GENERATION, NOT AT TERRAIN GENERATION, AND THAT
IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. The engine runs its sections in a fixed order - land,
elevation, cliffs, terrain, connections, objects - regardless of the order they
appear in the file. Elevation therefore runs BEFORE terrain generation, so a
terrain painted there does not exist yet when the elevation commands look for
it, and every one of them would match nothing. A land carries its own terrain
and is laid before elevation, which is why the split is a second land here.

  dirt half  - the command once.
  snow half  - the identical command five times.

  maximum model -> both halves peak at the same height. Repetition changed
                   coverage, not altitude.
  summing model -> the snow half runs far higher than the dirt half and pins at
                   the ceiling, while the dirt half sits at the asked-for height.

This is a single-map discriminator, which matters: the two halves see the same
seed, the same map size and the same land, so nothing but the repetition differs.

PREDICTION, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN
Maximum. The corpus map that prompted this makes no sense under summing - an
author does not write a height-7 command five times intending 35 - and terrain
already treats repetition as coverage. If the run says summing, then the
corpus map really is asking for the ceiling and Sec.6.2's original text was
right, and the fix shipped on 2026-08-07 needs reverting rather than refining.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWO RUNS.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --rows ELEVATION --bands 20
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT
  The reading is the MAXIMUM height on each half and the height histogram within
  each half, not the mean - a coverage difference moves the mean under both
  models and only the peak separates them.

Read the coverage too, because it is the other half of the claim. Under the
maximum model the snow half should be MORE COVERED than the dirt half at the
same peak height, which is what "repetition is a coverage idiom" actually
predicts. Equal peaks with equal coverage would mean the extra four commands did
nothing at all, which is a third outcome and is not what either model says.
*/
